ses that employ tipped workers in D.C."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Hotels, restaurants, and casinos employ tipped workers who would benefit from the tip deduction, affecting labor costs and compensation structures."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would overturn a District of Columbia law that separated D.C. tax rules from recent federal tax changes. If passed, it would force D.C. to adopt federal provisions including a higher standard deduction, new deductions for tips and overtime pay, a deduction for taxpayers over 65, and accelerated business depreciation rules. The resolution would also eliminate D.C.-specific tax provisions, including the D.C. child tax credit that the city had restored.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who files a tax return. Anyone who pays federal tax.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S001214","score":0.013644472543307301},"overlapCount":2},"meaning":{"percentile":27.847101563734007,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[{"kind":"sponsor","text":"They wrote this bill. Sponsoring a bill about your own district's industry is the ordinary case, not the odd one."}],"unattributedShare":0.7974326614092035}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"G000603","cycle":2026,"score":0.005413539631031854,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":19500,"donorShare":0.011145522769771463,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.005413539631031854}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7974326614092035},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, increasing demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Homeland Security to detain individuals who are in the country without authorization and have been arrested for or charged with burglary, theft, larceny, or shoplifting. It would also allow state governments to sue the federal government for injunctive relief when federal immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing individuals from custody or not fulfilling visa or asylum processing requirements—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S000250","score":0.02595346470272257},"overlapCount":50},"meaning":{"percentile":25.904280524403728,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[{"kind":"state-industry","text":"Lawyers & Lobbyists is also the top donor sector for 2 other members from TX. That points to the industry being big in the state, not to anything about this member."}],"unattributedShare":0.7974326614092035}},{"billId":"119-hjres-75","bioguideId":"G000603","cycle":2026,"score":0.003063964645410062,"matches":[{"industry":"retail-consumer","donorAmount":11000,"donorShare":0.006287217972691594,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0017521755005861822},{"industry":"hospitality","donorAmount":6000,"donorShare":0.0034293916214681425,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.0008432930216724942},{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":5000,"donorShare":0.002857826351223452,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0004684961231513856}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7974326614092035},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-75","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"75","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Office of Energy Efficiency and Renewable Energy, Department of Energy relating to \"Energy Conservation Program: Energy Conservation Standards for Commercial Refrigerators, Freezers, and Refrigerator-Freezers\".","introducedDate":"2025-03-10","latestActionDate":"2025-05-09","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-9.","policyArea":"Energy","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Energy","Energy efficiency and conservation","Food industry and services","Lighting, heating, cooling"],"sponsorBioguideId":"G000601","cosponsorCount":13,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/75","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Manufacturers of commercial refrigeration equipment would no longer need to meet the stricter energy efficiency standards that were set to take effect in 2029."},{"industry":"retail-consumer","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Grocery stores, restaurants, and other food retailers that purchase and operate commercial refrigeration equipment would be affected by whether they must buy equipment meeting the new standards."},{"industry":"hospitality","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Hotels and restaurants use commercial refrigeration equipment and would be affected by the regulatory requirements for equipment they purchase."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Electric utilities would see different demand patterns depending on whether commercial refrigeration equipment must meet higher efficiency standards."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution would cancel a Department of Energy rule that set stricter energy efficiency standards for commercial refrigerators, freezers, and similar equipment used by businesses. The rule, published in January 2025, would have required this equipment to meet new energy conservation standards by January 2029. If this resolution passes, those updated standards would be eliminated and the equipment would not need to comply with the new requirements.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys food.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"N000189","score":0.0257116644139778},"overlapCount":14},"meaning":{"percentile":14.152582530405938,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7974326614092035}},{"billId":"119-hjres-20","bioguideId":"G000603","cycle":2026,"score":0.001972460540452265,"matches":[{"industry":"real-estate","donorAmount":14000,"donorShare":0.008001913783425666,"billConfidence":0.4,"contribution":0.001255202162105987},{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":5000,"donorShare":0.002857826351223452,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0005603581080830299},{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":1000,"donorShare":0.0005715652702446905,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.00015690027026324838}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7974326614092035},"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-20","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"20","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Department of Energy relating to \"Energy Conservation Program: Energy Conservation Standards for Consumer Gas-fired Instantaneous Water Heaters\".","introducedDate":"2025-01-15","latestActionDate":"2025-05-09","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-6.","policyArea":"Energy","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of Energy","Energy efficiency and conservation","Lighting, heating, cooling","Oil and gas"],"sponsorBioguideId":"P000609","cosponsorCount":29,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/20","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Manufacturers of gas-fired instantaneous water heaters would be directly affected by the nullification of energy efficiency standards that govern their products."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The natural gas industry has a stake in standards that affect the efficiency and market viability of gas-fired appliances."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Gas utilities that supply fuel for these water heaters would be affected by standard